    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the midpoint of a line segment with endpoints $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$, we use the midpoint formula:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right).\n\\]\nFor the given endpoints $(6, 12)$ and $(0, -6)$, we can substitute these values into the formula to find the midpoint coordinates. Let's do this step by step using Python to ensure accuracy.\n\nFirst, we calculate the midpoint's x-coordinate:\n\\[\nx_{\\text{mid}} = \\frac{6 + 0}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3.\n\\]\nNext, we calculate the midpoint's y-coordinate:\n\\[\ny_{\\text{mid}} = \\frac{12 + (-6)}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3.\n\\]\nSo the midpoint is $(3, 3)$. The sum of the coordinates of the midpoint is:\n\\[\n3 + 3 = 6.\n\\]\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the coordinates of the endpoints\nx1, y1 = 6, 12\nx2, y2 = 0, -6\n\n# Calculate the midpoint coordinates\nx_mid = (x1 + x2) / 2\ny_mid = (y1 + y2) / 2\n\n# Calculate the sum of the coordinates of the midpoint\nsum_of_coordinates = x_mid + y_mid\nprint(sum_of_coordinates)\n```\n```output\n6.0\n```\nThe sum of the coordinates of the midpoint of the segment with endpoints $(6, 12)$ and $(0, -6)$ is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
